# How Godot's VoxelGI Lights a Room {#video subtitle="Baked voxel global illumination in Godot 4.7, followed through the source at the engine's sizes" engineVersion=4.7.2-stable commit=ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079 date=2026-10-31 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where VoxelGI runs {#c1 number=1 target=290 summary="What VoxelGI adds, the problem it solves, the parts it is made of, and what decides whether it runs." sources="frame-order, voxelgi-gate, voxelgi-doc, mobile-stub, gles3-stub, node-defaults, quality-setting"}

### VoxelGI adds the light that bounced off the room {#c1-room kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {voxelgiMix 0.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 18.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 5.2} {pose 20.0 17.0 6.3}}}}}}}

This is the lab's study room, lit only by its ceiling panel and the sky.

A surface the panel cannot reach directly gets only the sky's flat, even light.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Now Godot's VoxelGI adds the light that bounces around the room"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The red wall warms the sphere and the floor beside it, and the corners fill in"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The light that reaches a surface after bouncing comes from every other surface it can see"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.3}}
    "Gathering that light ray by ray, for every pixel in every frame, would cost far too much"}.

${cue "key" {' {cellView 0.0} {cutHeight 1.6}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "So VoxelGI cuts the room into a grid of small boxes, called cells, ahead of time"}.

${cue "key" {' {cellView 4.0}}}${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.3 1.0}}
    "It then lights those cells"}.

Every pixel reads their light back through a few wide cones, shapes that widen as they go.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 2.0}}
    "One sample of a cone averages many cells at once, so a pixel needs only a few samples"}.

### Baked once {#c1-map-bake kind=2d graph=frame focus="BAKE, SDF"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bake-entry"}}}
That ahead-of-time step is called the bake, and it usually runs once, in Godot's editor.

It turns the meshes inside the VoxelGI's box into an octree, a tree of cells in which each cell splits into eight smaller ones.

### And a distance field {#c1-map-sdf kind=2d graph=frame focus="SDF"}

It also makes a distance field, which records how far each place is from the nearest surface.

Later, that field helps find which cells are in shadow.

### Lit when something changes {#c1-map-light kind=2d graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX, DYN"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}
At run time, compute passes called the light passes light every cell and store the result in a three-dimensional texture.

The light passes run again only when a light changes, or the VoxelGI's data or settings change, so a still room costs them nothing.

### Moving objects, every frame {#c1-map-dynamic kind=2d graph=frame focus="DYN"}

Moving objects are left out of the bake, so they are drawn into that texture every frame instead.

### Read every frame {#c1-map-read kind=2d graph=frame focus="PRE, CONE"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-gi"}}}
Every frame, a compute pass called the screen pass traces cones through that texture for each pixel inside the box.

### Into the image {#c1-map-opaque kind=2d graph=frame focus="OPAQUE"}

Then the opaque pass, which shades the scene's solid surfaces, mixes the cones' light into the image as their indirect light.

By the end, you will be able to follow one pixel's light back through each of these parts.

