### Forward+ only, on whenever a VoxelGI is in view {#c1-gate kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues 1.0 {' {focusRow 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 16.0 18.0} {aim 8.0 22.0} {aim 14.0 19.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxelgi-doc"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Godot has three renderers, and VoxelGI works only in the one called Forward+"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gles3-stub"}}}The Compatibility renderer leaves its VoxelGI functions empty.

${cue "key" {' {callout "mobile-stub"}}}The Mobile renderer never links an object to a VoxelGI, so no surface there reads its light.

No project setting turns VoxelGI on or off.

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxelgi-gate"}}}${overText
    {' {showBoxRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Instead, its work runs in any frame where culling, the test of what is in view, finds a VoxelGI"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "node-defaults"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 4.0}}
    "A VoxelGI is a node placed in the scene"}.

By default it is a box twenty metres on each side.

${cue "key" {' {cutHeight 1.6} {cellView 0.0}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 4.0 7.0}}
    "That box is cut into one hundred twenty-eight cells along its longest axis"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "quality-setting"}}}${over
    {' {focusRowRange 4.0 12.0}}
    {overText
        {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
        "Besides the cells, one project setting picks how many cones each pixel traces, four by default, or six for a finer result"}}.

${overText {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}} "The other settings arrive in the chapters that use them"}.

