## Baking the octree {#c2 number=2 target=320 summary="The bake makes the box a cube of cells, sends every triangle down an octree, and samples it in each leaf it reaches." sources="bake-entry, find-meshes, begin-bake, plot-mesh, plot-face-recurse, tri-box, leaf-scan, material-cache"}

### The bake comes first {#c2-map kind=2d graph=frame focus=BAKE}

Everything VoxelGI knows about the room comes from one bake, so this chapter starts there.

### Static meshes inside the box are baked {#c2-collect kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelValues 1.0 {'
    {showBox 1.0}
    {boundsStep 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cellView 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {' {pose 30.0 32.0 11.0} {pose 44.0 34.0 10.0} {pose 36.0 30.0 10.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bake-entry"}}}${overText
    {' {showBoxRange 0.4 1.0}}
    "Pressing Bake starts the voxelizer, the code that turns the meshes in the VoxelGI's box into cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "find-meshes"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 3.0}}
    "The voxelizer first walks the scene, starting at the VoxelGI's parent node, and collects the meshes to bake"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "A mesh is collected when it is visible and its bounds touch the box"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "Each object also has a GI mode, and a mesh is baked only when that mode is Static, the default"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 6.0 7.0}}
    "Objects marked Dynamic are not baked, and chapter six follows them"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-mesh"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 2.2}}
    "Of those meshes, only the triangle surfaces are baked"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.2 1.2}}
    "A triangle that misses the box is skipped, and one that crosses its edge is kept whole, not clipped"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 2.2}}
    "The lab leaves the ceiling panel's mesh out of the bake, so the panel has no cells"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.2 3.2}}
    "The panel's light comes from an area light instead, which chapter four follows"}.

### The box becomes a cube of cells {#c2-cube kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounds panelValues 1.0 {' {showBox 1.0} {boundsStep 0.0} {focusRow 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 28.0 30.0 14.0} {pose 40.0 36.0 16.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "begin-bake"}}}${over
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {overText
        {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 1.0}}
        "At the default subdivision, the box's longest axis gets one hundred twenty-eight cells, two to the seventh"}}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingBeginBake {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "shrink"}}}}${overText
    {' {boundsStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A shorter axis gets fewer cells"}.

Its count is halved, and halved again, while half the longest side still covers the axis.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The box is three point two metres tall, half its six point four metre width, so its height gets sixty-four cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "cube"}}}}${overText
    {' {boundsStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Then every side is stretched to the longest one, so the octree covers a cube"}.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 2.0 4.0}} "So the octree's cells are cubes of one size"}.

The smallest are five centimetres wide, seven levels below the whole cube.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "begin-bake" {' "cell"}}}}The height's sixty-four cells stop at three point two metres.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 4.0 3.0}} "So the top half of that cube holds no cells at all"}.

### A triangle goes down the octree {#c2-descent kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelCode 0.5 {'
    listingPlotFace
    {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "octants"}}
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {descentLevel 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {satStep 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {path {' cameraDistance cameraYaw} {' {' 5.0 50.0} {' 2.2 58.0} {' 0.9 60.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {descentLevelRange 0.0 0.5}} "Take one triangle of the teapot's spout"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-face-recurse"}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 0.5 0.75}}
    "The triangle enters the octree at its root, the whole cube"}.

${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 0.75 1.0}}
    "The root is cut into eight children, one for each octant"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "sat"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Each child is tested against the triangle"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "create"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Only the children the triangle overlaps become cells, so the triangle carves a path one level deeper at a time"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "plot-face" {' "down"}}}}${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 3.0 7.0}}
    "Seven levels down, the children are five centimetres wide, and these smallest cells are called leaves"}.

${over
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 0.7}}
        "The triangle has created only the handful of cells it touches"}}.

### The test: a separating axis {#c2-sat kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {descentLevel 5.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {satStep 0.0}
    {focusRow 8.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.15 0.15 -1.8}}
    {cameraDistance 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 52.0 22.0} {aim 64.0 28.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "tri-box"}}}${overText
    {' {satStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The overlap test looks for a separating axis between the box and the triangle"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 1.2 sentence}}}On such an axis, the two shapes' projections do not overlap, so the shapes cannot touch.

${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "The test tries nine axes made from the triangle's edges, then the box's own three, then the triangle's plane"}.

${overText
    {' {descentLevelRange 6.0 5.0}}
    "The first axis that separates them rules the child out, so no cell is made there"}.

${overText
    {' {satStepRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "The readout panel names the axis that rejected the child drawn in red"}.

### Every triangle, sharing the cells {#c2-all kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelValues 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 3.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {focusRow 3.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 28.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 30.0 7.2} {pose 32.0 26.0 6.6} {pose 26.0 28.0 7.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "Every triangle of every mesh goes down the octree the same way"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 0.0 3.0}} "Triangles that reach the same cell share it"}.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 7.0}} "Level by level, the cells shrink and multiply"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 7.0}}
    "The room ends with forty-two thousand two hundred eighty-nine leaves, out of fifty-six thousand cells in all"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 0.7}} "The cells follow only the surfaces"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.7 1.4}}
    "So only about four in a hundred of the grid's million places hold a leaf"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.4 2.0}}
    "An octree like this, with most of its space left empty, is called sparse"}.

### A leaf samples its triangle on four by four columns {#c2-leaf kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelCode 0.2 {'
    listingLeafScan
    {highlightCode "leaf-scan" {' "axis"}}
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {workedLeaf 1.0}
    {leafColumns 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.32}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 50.0 22.0} {aim 62.0 30.0} {aim 56.0 26.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "leaf-scan"}}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At a leaf, the voxelizer picks the axis the triangle's plane faces most"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "leaf-scan" {' "columns"}}}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "The cell is cut into a four-by-four grid of columns along that axis"}.

${overText {' {leafColumnsRange 8.0 16.0}} "Each column the triangle crosses gives one sample"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 16.0 12.0}}
    "A sample adds the material's albedo, its emission, and the triangle's normal at that point to the leaf's sums"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 12.0 8.0}}
    "Each sample is scaled by a sixteenth, one share for each of the sixteen columns"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 8.0 12.0}}
    "So a triangle that covers half the leaf adds only half the weight of one that covers all of it"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 12.0 14.0}}
    "The leaf's alpha adds up those shares, so it is the leaf's coverage, the fraction of columns hit"}.

${overText
    {' {leafColumnsRange 14.0 16.0}}
    "The next chapter uses that alpha to turn the sums into an average"}.

### Materials become flat colours {#c2-material kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 0.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.8}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 24.0 6.4} {pose 28.0 30.0 6.9}}}}}}}

The colours the leaves receive come from the materials.

${cue "key" {' {callout "material-cache"}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "Each material is read once into two tables, each one hundred twenty-eight texels a side"}.

${overText {' {cellViewRange 0.0 1.0}} "One table holds the albedo, in linear colour"}.

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The other holds the emission, multiplied by its energy and the bake's exposure"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.0}} "Here each leaf shows the albedo it received"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.2}}
    "The room's materials are flat colours, so every sample of one mesh is the same"}.

### Next: finishing and packing the cells {#c2-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoOctree panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedTriangle 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 1.0}
    {cutHeight 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 26.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.8}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 26.0 6.4} {' 16.0 7.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "So the bake keeps only the cells that surfaces pass through"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.0 3.2}}
    "Each leaf sums what its triangles left in it, shown here as their coverage"}.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 6.0}} "Those sums are not yet what the graphics card reads"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 6.0 5.0}}
    "The next chapter finishes the cells and packs them into bytes"}.

### Where to read {#c2-sources kind=2d sources="bake-entry, find-meshes, begin-bake, plot-mesh, plot-face-recurse, tri-box, leaf-scan, material-cache"}

These are the voxelizer's functions, in the order the bake calls them.

## What the bake stores {#c3 number=3 target=300 summary="Leaves are averaged, parents emptied, the cells sorted and packed into 48 bytes, and a distance field made; then everything is uploaded." sources="fixup-leaf, fixup-parent, sort-cells, export-octree, export-data, rgbe9995, sdf-edt, sdf-encode, allocate-data, instance-texture"}

### From cells to bytes {#c3-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="BAKE, SDF"}

The bake has left a tree of cells that hold raw sums.

Before the graphics card sees them, the cells are finished, sorted, and packed.

### A leaf becomes an average {#c3-fixup kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.2 {'
    {fixupStep 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cutHeight 0.3}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.7}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 50.0 20.0 0.6} {pose 60.0 26.0 0.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fixup-leaf"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "This teapot leaf holds the sums from every triangle that reached it"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}} "Its alpha is the total of those triangles' shares"}.

${overText {' {fixupStepRange 0.0 1.0}} "Finishing the leaf divides every sum by that alpha"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 6.0}}
    "So the leaf now holds the average albedo and normal of its samples"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 6.0 9.0}}
    "After the division, its alpha is set to one, so every leaf counts as fully covered"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingFixup {highlightCode "fixup" {' "fight"}}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "Sometimes the samples' normals point in opposite directions and cancel out"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.3 0.4}}
    "When their average is shorter than one hundredth, the leaf keeps no normal at all"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 0.4 0.5}} "The light passes then treat that leaf as facing every way"}.

### Parents keep only their coverage {#c3-parents kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelCode 1.0 {'
    listingFixup
    {highlightCode "fixup" {' "parent"}}
    {octreeLevel 6.0}
    {cellView 1.0}
    {cutHeight 1.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.5 -0.6}}
    {cameraYaw 28.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 28.0 5.0} {pose 36.0 34.0 5.6}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fixup-parent"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 6.0}}
    "A parent cell, one that has children, gets no colour at all"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 1.0}}
    "Its albedo, emission, and normal are all set to zero, because the bake never stores light in a parent"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "fixup" {' "alpha"}}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 0.8}}
    "A parent keeps only its alpha, its coverage, which is the sum of its children's alpha divided by eight"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 0.8 0.5}} "So a parent with four leaves inside it holds one half"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 6.0 4.0}}
    "The coarser the level, the more of a cell is open air, so fewer of its children exist and its coverage drops"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.5 1.6}}
    "The coarse levels get their light later, rebuilt from the leaves on the graphics card every time the lights change"}.

### Sorted by level, each level in one run {#c3-sort kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelCode 0.0 {'
    listingSortCells
    {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "key"}}
    {cellArray 1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.9 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 22.0 30.0} {aim 30.0 32.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "The cells are then sorted by level first, and then by position"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "root"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the root comes first, at index zero"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "remap"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "Because the cells moved, each parent's list of child indices is rewritten to point at their new places"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "sort-cells" {' "runs"}}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 7.0}}
    "Each level now fills one unbroken run of the array, shown here in its own colour"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "level-counts"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    "The bake also hands over how many cells each level has"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 4.0}}
    "The renderer, the part of Godot that draws each frame, uses those counts to find where each level's run starts and ends"}.

### Forty-eight bytes a cell, in two records {#c3-bytes kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.2 {'
    {cellArray 0.0}
    {fixupStep 1.0}
    {focusRow 1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 0.0}
    {cutHeight 0.3}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.7}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 54.0 20.0 0.6} {pose 62.0 26.0 0.8} {pose 56.0 22.0 0.7}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "export-octree"}}}Each cell is stored as two records.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The first record holds its eight children's indices, thirty-two bytes"}.

An index of all ones means that child is empty.

${cue "key" {' {callout "export-data"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The second record is sixteen bytes"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "It starts with the cell's position, counted in that level's own cells"}.

The three coordinates are packed into eleven, ten, and eleven bits.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 4.0 5.0}}
    "Then come its albedo and coverage, one byte for each of the four channels"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "rgbe9995"}}}${over
    {' {focusRowRange 6.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cellViewRange 0.0 3.0}}
        "Its emission is three nine-bit numbers that share one exponent, so emission brighter than white still fits in four bytes"}}.

${overText {' {cellViewRange 3.0 2.0}} "This room has no emission, so those bytes are zero here"}.

${overText {' {focusRowRange 8.0 9.0}} "Last comes its normal, as three signed bytes, one per axis"}.

### The distance field, exact, in three passes {#c3-field kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoField panelValues 0.0 {' {slice 1.0} {edtPass 0.0} {sliceHeight 0.3}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-edt"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.6 0.45}}
    "The bake also measures, on a grid of leaf-sized places, how far each place is from the nearest leaf"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.45 0.3}}
    "That distance field will let shadow rays skip through empty space"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingDistanceField {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "seed"}}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Leaves start at a distance of zero, and every other place starts far away, shown white"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "z"}}}}${overText
    {' {edtPassRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Then a pass runs along every column of cells in the z direction, and finds each place's distance within its column"}.

${over
    {' {edtPassRange 1.0 3.0}}
    {cat {'
        {cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "y"}}}}
        {text "It runs again along y, "}
        {cue "key" {' {highlightCode "distance-field" {' "x"}}}}
        {text "and then along x"}}}}.

Together, these three passes are called an exact distance transform, because they give each place its exact distance.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-encode"} panelValues}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Each place keeps its distance in a single byte"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "A leaf stores zero, and any other place stores its distance in whole cells plus one, so zero always marks a leaf"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.3 1.0}}
    "At the height of the sphere's centre, the field forms a ring around each object"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 1.0 0.6}}
    "In the next chapter, shadow rays use those distances to jump through empty space"}.

### What the GPU keeps {#c3-upload kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelValues 0.5 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 0.0}
    {sliceHeight 0.0}
    {cellArray 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.4 0.0}}
    {cameraYaw 10.0}
    {cameraPitch 60.0}
    {cameraDistance 8.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 0.0 70.0} {aim 16.0 58.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "allocate-data"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceRange 2.0 1.0}}
    "The upload turns the two records into two storage buffers"}.

It turns the field into a three-dimensional texture, with one byte per texel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-gpu-disabled"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Godot's source also holds a graphics-card version of the field, but it is compiled out"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 7.0 sentence}}}So the field the bake made on the processor is the one used.

${cue "key" {' {callout "instance-texture"}}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each VoxelGI in the renderer then gets a colour texture the size of the octree, for the light passes to fill"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "That texture has one mip for each octree level, each mip a half-size copy of the one before"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Beside it is a buffer of four floats per cell, which will hold each cell's light at full precision"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 3.0 1.0}} "In the lab, the texture's finest mip takes four megabytes"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The cells take under four megabytes, and the distance field one megabyte"}.

### Next: light {#c3-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCells panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {slice 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 2.0}
    {cutHeight 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.5 -0.6}}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.4}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 32.0 5.0} {' 20.0 5.6}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 2.0 0.0}}
    "So every leaf now stores what its surface is, its albedo and normal"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The distance field stores how far each place is from the nearest leaf"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 2.0 2.6}} "None of it is light yet"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 2.6 3.2}} "The next chapter lights the leaves"}.

### Where to read {#c3-sources kind=2d sources="fixup-leaf, fixup-parent, sort-cells, export-octree, export-data, rgbe9995, sdf-edt, sdf-encode, allocate-data, instance-texture"}

These are the lines that finish, pack, measure, and upload the cells.

## Lighting the cells {#c4 number=4 target=275 summary="When the lights change, each leaf gets its direct light, shadowed through the field, and the texture stores it over the dynamic range." sources="relight-gate, light-cache, max-lights, light-upload, compute-light, light-at-pos, raymarch, area-light, write-texture"}

### Light passes, on change {#c4-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX"}

The cells now hold what each surface is.

The light passes, the compute passes from chapter one, give each cell the light it receives.

### Only when the data or a light changes {#c4-when kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {lightX 0.9}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {focusRow -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 18.0 4.6} {pose 12.0 24.0 4.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}The light passes do not run every frame.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange -1.0 0.0}}
    "They run only when a light changes, when the bake is redone, or when one of the VoxelGI's settings changes"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-cache"}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.4}}
    "The culler, the code that decides what is in view, compares every light each frame with a copy of that light's settings, saved earlier"}.

${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.4 -0.6}}
    "Move a light, and the cells are lit again, here the floor under its new position"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "max-lights"}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange -0.6 0.9}}
    "At most thirty-two lights take part, the engine's limit"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-upload"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each light's position and range are first converted into the box's cells, so the passes can work in cell units"}.

### A cell's direct light, step by step {#c4-direct kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 0.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 2.0 16.0} {aim 10.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "compute-light"}}}${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 1.8 1.6}}
    "Take this floor cell beside the sphere, the ringed one"}.

${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 1.6 1.4}}
    "The lab adds an extra omni light here, a point light that shines in every direction"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingComputeLight {highlightCode "compute-light" {' "vector"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The pass first finds the direction from the cell to the light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "omni-atten"} panelValues}}${over
    {' {lightStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    {overText
        {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.5}}
        "Then it finds how much the light falls off over the distance between them"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-at-pos"}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "A cell that faces away from the light gets nothing from it"}.

${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.5 0.9}}
    "Otherwise, the light is weighted by the cosine between the cell's normal and the direction to the light"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingComputeLight {highlightCode "compute-light" {' "result"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "That light times the cell's albedo, plus the cell's emission, is the light the cell sends back out"}.

### The shadow ray steps through the distance field {#c4-shadow kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingRaymarch
    {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "loop"}}
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 16.0 4.4} {pose 8.0 20.0 4.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "raymarch"}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "When the light casts shadows, a ray starts on the light's side and marches back toward the cell through the distance field"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "read"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "At each step, the ray reads from the field how far the nearest surface is, shown as the circle"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "step"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 2.0 4.0}}
    "Then it jumps that far"}.

The jump is rounded down to whole cells, along the axis the ray mostly follows.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "solid"}}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 4.0 20.0}}
    "Here the ray reaches the sphere's cells and reads a solid cell, where the field is zero"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 20.0 40.0}}
    "So the floor cell is in shadow, and it gets none of this light"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "raymarch" {' "partial"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightZRange -0.4 -0.25}}
    "But close to a surface, the field reads less than one cell, and then the cell's light is only dimmed"}.

${overText {' {lightZRange -0.25 -0.1}} "That gives the shadow a slightly soft edge"}.

### The panel's area light has no shadow {#c4-area kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.0 1.2 -0.5}}
    {cameraDistance 5.6}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 0.0 16.0} {aim 10.0 22.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "area-light"}}}${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The ceiling panel is an area light, a glowing rectangle rather than a point"}.

${overText
    {' {lightStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "Its light on a cell is an integral over the whole rectangle"}.

${overText {' {lightStepRange 3.0 4.0}} "VoxelGI does not sum the rectangle point by point"}.

It evaluates the integral in closed form, with a standard fit called linearly transformed cosines.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.7}}
    "The area light never marches a ray through the distance field"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.7 1.2}}
    "So the panel lights the cells under the sphere as if the sphere were not there"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ltc"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.2 0.2}}
    "Godot's direct lighting divides this integral by two pi"}.

The code shows that VoxelGI leaves that division out, so the panel lights the cells two pi times as brightly as direct lighting would.

### Every leaf, lit {#c4-all kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 32.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 38.0 7.2}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 1.6}}
    "Every leaf runs the same pass, sixty-four leaves to a workgroup on the graphics card"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.6 3.2}} "The room's cells take on the panel's light"}.

${overText {' {cellViewRange 5.0 1.0}} "Cells the panel sees are bright"}.

${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 1.0 5.0}}
    "Cells it cannot see stay dark, waiting for the bounce in the next chapter"}.

### The texture stores light over the dynamic range {#c4-write kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {dynamicRange 2.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.1 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 14.0}
    {cameraPitch 48.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 8.0 50.0} {aim 20.0 44.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "write-texture"} {focusRow 2.0}}}${overText
    {' {cellViewRange 5.0 4.0}}
    "A last pass, the write pass, stores each cell's light in that cell's texel of the three-dimensional texture"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.0 sentence}}}Each channel of a texel has only eight bits, for values from zero to one.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the pass first divides the light by a scale called the dynamic range"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "The dynamic range is two by default, so light up to twice white survives"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicRangeRange 2.0 1.0}}
    "At a range of one, the bright cells under the panel clip to white, and the readout's count of clipped leaves climbs"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicRangeRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The alpha channel stores the cell's coverage, one for a leaf and less for a sparse parent"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.6}}
    "Texels with no cell stay at zero, fully transparent, so a cone sees straight through them"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "unused-push"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.6 0.8}}
    "Godot's VoxelGI stores no direction with its light"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.8 1.0}}
    "The code shows that the pass still receives an anisotropy value, but no line reads it, so that value changes nothing"}.

### Next: the bounce {#c4-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 5.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 26.0 6.6} {' 14.0 7.2}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.8}} "So every leaf now holds the direct light it sends out"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.8 0.4}}
    "Every shadow in that light comes from marching the distance field, and the area light casts none"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.4 1.8}}
    "So far, each cell has only light that came straight from a light"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.8 3.2}} "The next chapter lets the cells light each other"}.

### Where to read {#c4-sources kind=2d sources="relight-gate, light-cache, max-lights, light-upload, compute-light, light-at-pos, raymarch, area-light, write-texture"}

These are the light pass's lines, from the check that decides when it runs to the texture.

## The second bounce and the mips {#c5 number=5 target=205 summary="The coarse levels are filled from their children, then six cones from every leaf gather the light around it." sources="update-mipmaps, pass-order, second-bounce, bounce-cones, bounce-march, bounce-restore"}

### The texture reads itself back {#c5-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="LIGHT, TEX"}

One pass of light reaches only what the lights see.

A second pass reads back the texture the first pass wrote, so the cells light each other.

### A parent's light from its children {#c5-mips kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMips panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingUpdateMipmaps
    {highlightCode "update-mipmaps" {' "children"}}
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 0.0}
    {workedParent 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 6.0} {' -6.0} {' 4.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-mipmaps"}}}${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.4 0.2}}
    "A cone widens as it travels, so it must read coarser copies of the light"}.

${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.2 0.0}}
    "The bake left the coarse levels without light, so a pass called the mip pass fills them first"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 1.0}} "Each parent sums the light of its children"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Then it divides that sum by a divisor, somewhere between eight and the number of children it really has"}.

${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "A setting called propagation picks where that divisor falls"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 3.0 4.0}} "Level by level, each mip holds a coarser copy of the light"}.

${overText {' {mipLevelRange 4.0 7.0}} "The last mip is a single texel for the whole box"}.

### Propagation sets the divisor {#c5-prop kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMips panelValues 0.3 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {mipLevel 2.0}
    {workedParent 1.0}
    {propagation 0.5}
    {focusRow 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' -4.0} {' 6.0}} beat}}}}}

${overText {' {propagationRange 0.5 0.25}} "At a propagation of zero, the divisor is always eight"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 0.25 0.0}}
    "So empty children count as black, and the coarse levels darken"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At a propagation of one, only the children that exist are averaged, so the coarse levels keep their brightness"}.

${overText
    {' {propagationRange 1.0 0.5}}
    "At the default of one half, a parent with four children divides by six, halfway between eight and four"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "Propagation is a choice between light that fades with distance and light that spreads too far"}.

### Six cones from every leaf {#c5-cones kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounce panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "frame"}}
    {workedBounce 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 6.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 28.0} {aim 48.0 32.0} {aim 40.0 30.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "second-bounce"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 2.0 6.0}}
    "Take this floor cell near the red wall, the ringed one"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "cones"}}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 4.0}}
    "The bounce pass, the second light pass, builds a set of three axes around the cell's normal"}.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "It sends one cone straight along the normal, with a weight of one quarter"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-cones"}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 1.0 6.0}}
    "Five more cones lean sixty degrees out at a weight of point one five each, so the six weights add up to one"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 3.0}} "Each cone opens thirty degrees from its axis"}.

### Each cone widens and reads coarser mips {#c5-march kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBounce panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "march"}}
    {workedBounce 1.0}
    {conesShown 1.0}
    {coneSteps 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 40.0 2.4} {' 30.0 3.2}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-march"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A cone starts one and a half cells from the cell's centre, clear of the cell itself"}.

${cue "key" {' runMarchCone {runSeconds 2.5} {runFrom 1.0} {runTo 6.0}}}The cone widens as it goes, and on each step it advances by half its current width.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "lod"}}}}At each step, it reads the mip whose texels match that width.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}Each sample is blended in behind what the cone has already seen, front to back, like layers of glass.

The cone stops once it is ninety-five percent opaque, or when it runs out of distance.

${cue "key" {' {stop {' "marchCone"}}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 1.0 6.0}}
    "The last cone here takes eleven steps and still ends only two thirds opaque"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 11.0}}
    "That is because the room's cells are thin shells around open air"}.

### The bounce is added, and everything rebuilt {#c5-restore kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelCode 1.0 {'
    listingSecondBounce
    {highlightCode "second-bounce" {' "restore"}}
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 0.2}
    {bounces 1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.4 0.4 0.2}}
    {cameraYaw 40.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 34.0 32.0 4.0} {pose 48.0 36.0 4.6}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-restore"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.2 0.5}}
    "Each cone's light is multiplied by its weight, and by the cell's albedo for the bounce off that cell"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.5 0.65}}
    "It is also multiplied by the dynamic range, which undoes the division the texture applied"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 0.65 0.8}} "The result is added to the cell's direct light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-order"}}}Then the mips are filled again, and the texture is written again.

${overText {' {bouncesRange 1.0 2.0}} "The floor cells beside the red wall brighten"}.

${overText {' {bouncesRange 2.0 1.0}} "Only the leaves trace bounce cones"}.

${overText {' {bouncesRange 1.0 2.0}} "They trace them only once, so there is no third bounce"}.

### Next: what moves {#c5-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cellView 4.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {bounces 2.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.6 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 34.0}
    {cameraDistance 7.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 28.0 6.6} {' 16.0 7.2}} beat}}}}}

${over
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    {overText
        {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.0}}
        "So the texture now holds two bounces of light, at every level from five-centimetre cells to the whole box"}}.

${overText {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 7.0}} "All of it was baked from objects that stand still"}.

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 1.0 3.2}} "The next chapter adds one that moves"}.

### Where to read {#c5-sources kind=2d sources="update-mipmaps, pass-order, second-bounce, bounce-cones, bounce-march, bounce-restore"}

These are the mip and bounce passes, and the order they run in.

## Moving objects {#c6 number=6 target=225 summary="An object marked Dynamic is drawn from six sides every frame, lit, shrunk, and plotted into the same texture." sources="gi-mode-flags, dynamic-list, dynamic-oversample, dynamic-views, material-outputs, dynamic-lighting, shrink, plot-blend, dynamic-clear"}

### Dynamic objects write into the texture {#c6-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="DYN, TEX"}

The bake knows only what stood still.

Baking again every frame would be far too slow.

So an object marked Dynamic is drawn into the texture every frame instead.

### Dynamic objects are not baked {#c6-mode kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 50.0 1.8} {' 60.0 2.2}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-mode-flags"}}}Here the teapot's GI mode is Dynamic, so the bake leaves it out.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The lab's room was baked without it, so the teapot has no cells and no baked light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-list"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "In every frame where the room's VoxelGI is in view, each of its dynamic objects is drawn into the texture"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.3}}
    "That happens whether the object moved or not, so it costs work even when the object stands still"}.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.6}} "The points on screen are the texels the teapot wrote"}.

Each is shown in the light it received, drawn brighter than the light stored.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.6 0.0}}
    "These points are only a display, because the lab's own shading still uses the bake without the teapot"}.

### Six views, sixteen texels a cell {#c6-views kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView 0.0}
    {shrinkLevel 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 48.0 18.0} {aim 62.0 24.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-oversample"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.15}}
    "The object's bounding box is drawn at sixteen texels per cell"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.15 0.0}}
    "That is fine enough to measure, later, how much of each cell the object covers"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-views"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 0.0 5.0}}
    "The object is drawn six times, with orthographic views along each axis in both directions, so no side is missed"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "material-outputs"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 5.0 0.0}}
    "Each view records the material's albedo, normal, and emission at every texel, along with its depth"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-lighting"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicViewRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "A texel's depth gives its place in the grid"}.

${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each texel is then lit like a cell, with shadows from the baked distance field alone"}.

### Shrunk two by two, plotted at each mip {#c6-shrink kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelCode 0.5 {'
    listingDynamicObject
    {highlightCode "dynamic-object" {' "shrink"}}
    {dynamicVoxels 0.0}
    {dynamicView 0.0}
    {shrinkLevel 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 54.0} {' 64.0} {' 56.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "shrink"}}}${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The lit view is then halved again and again, with each two-by-two block of texels becoming one"}.

${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "It goes from sixteen texels a cell to eight, then four, then two, then one"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "dynamic-object" {' "plot"}}}}${overText
    {' {shrinkLevelRange 3.0 4.0}}
    "At one texel a cell, a compute pass plots each texel, writing it into the texture's finest mip"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "plot-blend"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.0 0.5}}
    "A texel's depth usually falls between two cells along the view"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicVoxelsRange 0.5 1.0}}
    "So the texel is split between those two cells, by distance"}.

Each share is blended over what is already there.

${overText {' {shrinkLevelRange 4.0 11.0}} "Each further halving plots into the next, coarser mip"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "Each halving divides the light the way the mip pass does, by the divisor that propagation sets"}.

### The plotted voxels, mip by mip {#c6-mips kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicMip 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 46.0 18.0 1.8} {pose 58.0 24.0 2.4}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.3}}
    "At the finest mip, the teapot is drawn in five-centimetre cells"}.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 1.0}} "One mip up, the cells are ten centimetres wide"}.

${over
    {' {dynamicMipRange 1.0 3.0}}
    {overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.0}} "Each mip halves the detail again"}}.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 3.0 0.0}} "These coarser copies are what the wider cones will read"}.

### Rewritten every frame, never bounced {#c6-cost kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelValues 0.5 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicMip 0.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 50.0 20.0 1.6} {pose 64.0 26.0 2.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-clear"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.15}}
    "Before the teapot is plotted, the whole texture is cleared"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.15 0.3}}
    "Then the static light is written back from the float buffer, which keeps every cell's light at full precision"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "So the dynamic texels exist only for this frame, at every mip"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.3 0.4}}
    "The bounce already ran in the light passes, before the teapot was plotted"}.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.4 0.45}} "So no light bounces off the teapot"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.45 0.5}}
    "It casts no shadow either, because the baked distance field does not contain it"}.

${overText
    {' {dynamicMipRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "Here the cost is six draws and seventy-two compute dispatches per object, every frame"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-light-count"}}}${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.5 0.0}}
    "In chapter four, at most thirty-two lights took part"}.

The code shows that this pass has no such limit, so past thirty-two lights it reads beyond the end of the light buffer.

### Next: reading the texture {#c6-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoDynamic panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {dynamicVoxels 1.0}
    {dynamicView -1.0}
    {dynamicX 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 48.0 1.8} {' 62.0 2.4}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {dynamicXRange 0.0 0.4}}
    "So a moving object shows up in the same texture, redrawn every frame"}.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 0.0 1.0}} "The object is lit directly, but no light bounces off it"}.

${overText {' {dynamicXRange 0.4 0.0}} "Static or moving, everything ends in that one texture"}.

${overText {' {dynamicMipRange 1.0 0.0}} "The next chapter reads it back, one pixel at a time"}.

### Where to read {#c6-sources kind=2d sources="gi-mode-flags, dynamic-list, dynamic-oversample, dynamic-views, dynamic-lighting, shrink, plot-blend, dynamic-clear"}

These are the lines of the dynamic objects' path.

## Reading it per pixel {#c7 number=7 target=255 summary="The pre-pass tags each pixel with its VoxelGIs; the screen pass moves it into the box and traces four cones and a reflection cone." sources="pairing-two, instance-ubo, tag-write, process-gi, voxel-gi-compute, edge-blend, cones-low, cone-trace-45, cones-high, cone-trace, spec-cone, range-restore"}

### The screen pass reads the texture {#c7-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="PRE, CONE, TEX"}

The texture is now full of light.

The question is how a pixel reads that light cheaply, and the screen pass answers it every frame.

### Each pixel knows its VoxelGIs {#c7-tag kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.5 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {focusRow -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 36.0 14.0} {aim 46.0 18.0}}}}}}}

First, the screen pass needs to know which VoxelGIs light each pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pairing-two"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 30.0}}
    "Each object keeps the first two VoxelGIs it overlaps, so a third one never lights it"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "tag-write"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange -1.0 0.0}}
    "A pass called the depth pre-pass draws depth before the main shading"}.

It writes those two VoxelGIs' indices into every pixel it draws.

${overText {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 90.0}} "That takes two bytes per pixel"}.

An index of two hundred fifty-five means none.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 20.0}}
    "So the screen pass knows, for each pixel, which VoxelGIs to read"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "instance-ubo"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 90.0 60.0}}
    "Their settings are kept in one buffer, shared by up to eight VoxelGIs in view"}.

${overText {' {pixelElevationRange 20.0 10.0}} "This sphere's pixels name the room's only VoxelGI"}.

### Into the box's cells {#c7-space kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {pixelAzimuth 60.0}
    {focusRow 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 40.0 3.4} {' 32.0 3.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "voxel-gi-compute"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 10.0}}
    "Take this ringed pixel, on the side of the sphere that faces the red wall"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 100.0}}
    "The pass moves the pixel's position and normal into the box's cells"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 100.0 85.0}}
    "A pixel outside the box gets nothing from this VoxelGI"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "edge-blend"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "An edge fade then weights the light this VoxelGI gives the pixel"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 85.0 60.0}}
    "The fade is one at the box's centre and falls to zero at its faces"}.

${overText {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 20.0}} "The fade exists to blend two boxes that overlap"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 20.0 30.0}}
    "But with one box, the weighted light is then divided by that same fade, so the fade cancels out"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 30.0 40.0}}
    "Only a pixel exactly on a face, where the fade is zero, loses the box's light"}.

### Four cones at forty-five degrees {#c7-low kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}
    {focusRow 3.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 40.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 22.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-low"}}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 4.0}}
    "At the default quality, four cones leave the pixel at forty-five degrees from its normal, each with a weight of one quarter"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cone-trace-45"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "For a cone this wide, the radius at any point equals the distance travelled"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Each step advances by that radius, so it doubles the distance and reads one mip coarser, where a texel is twice as wide"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 2.0 6.0}}
    "That texel already averages everything in the cone's footprint, so one sample stands in for many"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 72.0}}
    "The samples are blended front to back, as in the bounce"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 72.0 85.0}}
    "The cone stops when its alpha reaches one, or when it leaves the box"}.

### Six cones at High {#c7-high kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 6.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}
    {quality 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 18.0} {aim 44.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-high"}}}${overText
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "At High quality, the pixel traces the bounce's six cones instead"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 16.0 10.0}}
    "Each of those cones is narrower, thirty degrees from its axis to its edge"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cone-trace"}}}${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 10.0 4.0}}
    "Each takes smaller steps, half its width at a time"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 4.0 16.0}}
    "So the six cones take more samples, each from a finer mip"}.

${overText {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}} "At the default, four wide cones do the work"}.

### One more cone for the reflection {#c7-spec kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {sphereRoughness 0.5}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 38.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "spec-cone"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 80.0}}
    "The reflection gets one more cone, along the mirror direction"}.

${overText {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.5 0.3}} "That cone's width follows the surface's roughness"}.

The pass squares the roughness first.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.3 0.15}}
    "On a smooth sphere, the cone is narrow and travels far"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.15 0.9}}
    "On a rough one, it opens seventy degrees from its axis"}.

${over
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.9 0.5}}
    {overText
        {' {pixelAzimuthRange 80.0 60.0}}
        "The sphere's reflection of the room follows the roughness, from sharp to blurred"}}.

### The range restored, the coverage kept {#c7-scale kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {interior 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 36.0} {' 44.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "range-restore"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 68.0}}
    "The cones' light is multiplied back by the dynamic range, which undoes the texture's division"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 68.0 75.0}}
    "It is also multiplied by the VoxelGI's energy, a brightness setting"}.

${cue "key" {' panelCode listingVoxelGiCompute {highlightCode "voxel-gi-compute" {' "interior"}}}}The cones' alpha says how much of their view they found filled.

${overText {' {interiorRange 0.0 1.0}} "With the interior setting on, that alpha is forced to one"}.

So the environment's light never mixes in.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}${overText
    {' {interiorRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "That alpha is stored with the light in the screen pass's buffers"}.

### A check that is always true {#c7-quirk kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelCode 0.3 {'
    listingVoxelGiCompute
    {highlightCode "voxel-gi-compute" {' "space"}}
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 40.0} {' 32.0}} beat}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "normal-length-quirk"}}}${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 15.0}}
    "Before any of this, the pass checks that the pixel's normal is valid"}.

${overText {' {pixelElevationRange 15.0 20.0}} "But that test is always true"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 20.0 25.0}}
    "In GLSL, Godot's shading language, a vector's length method returns its component count, three"}.

${overText {' {pixelElevationRange 25.0 28.0}} "So every pixel is traced, whatever its normal"}.

### Next: into the image {#c7-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw cameraDistance} {' {' 30.0 3.4} {' 44.0 3.8}} beat}}}}}

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 90.0}}
    "So every pixel traces four cones and a reflection cone through the texture"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 90.0 75.0}}
    "It also keeps how much of its view those cones found filled"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 75.0 60.0}}
    "The light and its alpha go into buffers, which the opaque pass turns into colour"}.

### Where to read {#c7-sources kind=2d sources="pairing-two, tag-write, process-gi, voxel-gi-compute, edge-blend, cones-low, cone-trace-45, cones-high, cone-trace, spec-cone, range-restore"}

These are the screen pass's lines, from the per-pixel tag to the scaled cones.

## Keeping it fast {#c8 number=8 target=420 summary="The bill, and how Godot keeps it small: light passes only on change, over occupied cells only, a level at a time; a few cone samples per pixel; half resolution." sources="relight-gate, update-gate, light-cache, sort-cells, level-dispatch, local-size, pass-order, raymarch, cones-low, cones-high, quality-doc, spec-cone, half-res-setting, half-res-buffers, half-res-shift, half-res-dispatch, gi-buffer-fetch, half-res-doc"}

### Three rates {#c8-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="BAKE, LIGHT, CONE"}

Before the opaque pass turns those buffers into colour, this chapter looks at the bill.

It asks what all of this costs, and how Godot keeps that cost small.

The work comes at three rates.

### Once {#c8-map-bake kind=2d graph=frame focus="BAKE"}

The bake runs once, in the editor.

### On change {#c8-map-light kind=2d graph=frame focus="LIGHT"}

The light passes run only when something changes.

### Every frame {#c8-map-cone kind=2d graph=frame focus="CONE"}

The screen pass runs every frame.

### Reading the panel {#c8-panel kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 1.0 {'
    unhighlight
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 0.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 12.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 24.0 20.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "scene pass"}}}}The panel on the right times the graphics card's work.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.5 0.52}}
    "Its line is each frame's total time, so a spike is a frame that did more work"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}Below the line, each row is one pass, averaged over the last frames.

A row shows the pass's name, a bar for its share of the frame, and its average time in milliseconds.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "scene pass" "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}The row called scene pass is the room's own shading, and it takes most of the frame.

The screen pass is the row named ${code "voxelgiGiCS"}, after its compute shader.

This chapter shows how Godot keeps that row small.

${cue "key" {' unhighlight}}These times are this machine's.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.52 0.5}}
    "Watch for the rows that appear only when their work actually runs"}.

### Light passes only on change {#c8-cache kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 1.0 {' unhighlight {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 20.0 16.0 6.6} {pose 30.0 20.0 5.8}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-gate"}}}Right now, no light pass is listed.

Nothing changed, so Godot does no light-pass work at all.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-cache"} {extraLight 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 0.9 0.2}}
    "Each frame, on the processor, the culler compares every visible light near the box with the copy it saved"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "relight-gate"}}}${overText
    {' {giDynamicRangeRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The light passes run only when a light, the data, or one of three settings has changed"}.

${overText
    {' {giPropagationRange 0.5 0.8}}
    "Those settings are the dynamic range, propagation, and the second bounce"}.

${cue "key" {' {giRelightEachFrame 1.0}}}To measure the light passes, the lab now relights the room every frame, which Godot does only when something changed.

${cue "key" {'
    {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiLightCS" "voxelgiBounceCS" "voxelgiMipmapsCS" "voxelgiWriteCS"}}}}Four rows appear, for the direct light, the bounce, the mips, and the writes.

${cue "key" {' {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiBounceCS"}}}}On this machine, the bounce takes the longest, because it traces six cones from every leaf that has a normal.

${cue "key" {' unhighlight}}}${overText
    {' {giRelightEachFrameRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "When the lab returns to Godot's behaviour, those rows are gone, so a still room's light costs nothing per frame"}.

### Only the occupied cells {#c8-sparse kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelValues 0.5 {'
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cutHeight 3.2}
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 18.0 24.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 30.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {focusRow 1.0}}}The readout on the right counts what one update of the light passes costs, and what one frame costs.

This chapter's numbers come from it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange -1.0 7.0}}
    "When the light passes do run, they visit only the cells the bake made"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "These are the cells shown here, not the empty grid around them"}.

${over
    {' {cutHeightRange 3.2 1.2}}
    {overText
        {' {focusRowRange 3.0 4.0}}
        {++
            {++ "That is " {rounded {leaves} 2}}
            " leaves, against more than a million texels in the texture's finest mip"}}}.

### A level at a time {#c8-levels kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelValues 0.5 {'
    {focusRow -1.0}
    {octreeLevel 7.0}
    {cutHeight 1.6}
    {giRelightEachFrame 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 22.0 28.0 6.4} {pose 30.0 32.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' insetCellArray imageCornerBottomLeft {callout "sort-cells"}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 1.6 2.0}}
    "The cells are stored sorted by level, with the root first and the leaves last"}.

${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 2.0 2.4}}
    "So each level is one run of the array, one colour in this bar"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "local-size"}}}${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Each run is one dispatch, one call to the graphics card, with sixty-four cells to a workgroup"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "level-dispatch"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 5.0}}
    "The first light pass begins with the leaves"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 5.0 3.0}}
    "Then the mip pass climbs one level per dispatch, with a barrier between dispatches"}.

${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 3.0 1.0}}
    "The barrier is there because a parent sums its children's light, so the children must be finished first"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pass-order"}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 1.0 7.0}}
    "Then eight writes, one for each level of the octree, copy the cells into the texture's mips"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 1.0}}
    f"{With the second bounce on, one update of this room takes ${spoken {dispatches}} dispatches}"}.

${cue "key" {' {imageHide {' "cellArray"}} {focusRow -1.0}}}${overText
    {' {octreeLevelRange 7.0 -1.0}}
    "The lab's light passes follow the same order on the graphics card"}.

### Shadows step by the field {#c8-march kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {workedLight 1.0}
    {lightStep 4.0}
    {marchSteps 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 0.0 16.0 4.4} {pose 8.0 20.0 4.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "raymarch"}}}${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Inside the light pass, a shadow ray reads the distance field"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "The field says how far the nearest surface is, so the ray can safely jump that far"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 3.0 12.0}}
    "So its steps are long in open air, and short only near a surface"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 12.0 26.0}}
    "A step is never shorter than one cell along the ray's main axis"}.

${overText
    {' {marchStepsRange 26.0 40.0}}
    "So the march is never slower than a walk from cell to cell"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-at-pos"}}}A light that is out of range, too dim, or behind the cell is skipped before any march starts.

### A few samples per pixel {#c8-cones kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelValues 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {coneSteps 0.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 34.0 18.0} {aim 44.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-low"}}}Everything so far runs only on change, but the screen pass runs every frame, so its price is paid per pixel.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 {float lowCones}}}
    f"{At the default quality, a pixel traces ${spoken lowCones} cones}"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 0.0 {float {lowSteps}}}}
    f"{In this box, each cone takes at most ${spoken {lowSteps}} steps, so a pixel takes at most ${spoken {* lowCones {lowSteps}}} samples}"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 3.0 5.0}}
    "Each sample reads a prefiltered mip as wide as the cone at that point"}.

${overText
    {' {coneStepsRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "That texel is already the average of the cells it covers, a cheap stand-in for many rays"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 6.0 7.0}} "The price is that thin walls blur"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cones-high"}}}${over
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {over
        {' {conesShownRange {float lowCones} {float highCones}}}
        {overText
            {' {coneStepsRange {float {lowSteps}} {float {highSteps}}}}
            f"{At High, ${spoken highCones} narrower cones widen more slowly}"}}}.

${cue "key" {' panelPerformance {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}}}So their steps are shorter and more numerous, and the screen pass's row grows.

${cue "key" {' {callout "quality-doc"} {specCone 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "The documentation says High also gives better reflections"}.

${overText
    {' {conesShownRange {float highCones} {float lowCones}}}
    "But only the diffuse cones for ambient light change, and the reflection cone stays the same"}.

### The reflection cone {#c8-spec kind=3d}

${cue "key" {'
    demoPixel
    {callout "spec-cone"}
    {inset ""}
    panelValues
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 0.0}
    {specCone 1.0}
    {sphereRoughness 1.0}
    {cameraDrift 0.3}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 38.0 16.0} {aim 30.0 20.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 1.0 0.5}}
    "That reflection cone is the one to watch, because its width follows the roughness"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.5 0.0}}
    f"{A fully rough surface takes at most ${spoken {roughSteps}} steps, and a mirror at most ${spoken {mirrorSteps}}}"}.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.0 0.04}}
    "A mirror's cone is nearly a ray, so it steps only half a cell at a time"}.

${overText {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.04 0.07}} "Steps that short never jump over a wall"}.

${overText {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.07 0.1}} "So smooth surfaces are the most expensive pixels"}.

### Half resolution {#c8-half kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelPerformance 0.3 {'
    {highlightPasses {' "voxelgiGiCS"}}
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 1.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}
    {quality 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.6 0.7 -0.4}}
    {cameraYaw 40.0}
    {cameraPitch 16.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {'
        {cameraPath {'
            {pose 40.0 16.0 3.4}
            {pose 44.0 17.0 3.0}
            {pose 40.0 15.0 1.7}
            {pose 36.0 16.0 1.6}
            {pose 40.0 16.0 3.2}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-setting"}}}One project setting, half resolution, trades quality for speed directly, and it is off by default.

On the left is the frame at full resolution, and on the right the frame with the setting on.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-buffers"}}}${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "The ambient and reflection buffers become half as wide and half as tall"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-shift"}}}Each of their texels traces only the top-left pixel of its two by two block.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-dispatch"}}}So only a quarter of the pixels are traced, and the screen pass's row shrinks with them.

${overText {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}} "Here are the ambient buffers at full and half size"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-buffer-fetch"}}}Without multisampling, the opaque pass reads the half-size one with a plain bilinear blend.

${cue "key" {' {giHalfResolution 0.0}}}That blend does not weight by depth.

${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "So along the sphere's edge, texels traced on the wall and on the sphere are blended together"}.

The edge turns ragged, in steps two pixels wide.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-res-doc"}}}These are the artifacts on polygon edges that the documentation warns of.

${cue "key" {' {viewMode 0.0}}}Each artifact is one half-size texel wide, so at a higher resolution they shrink.

### Next: into the image {#c8-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCost panelHidden 1.0 {'
    {giHalfResolution 0.0}
    {costSplit 0.0}
    {viewMode 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 6.2}}}}}}}

${overText {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.75}} "So VoxelGI pays for little each frame"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.75 0.5}}
    "Its light passes run only on change, and only over occupied cells"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.5 1.0}}
    "Each pixel takes a few cone samples, and half resolution traces only a quarter of the pixels when it is on"}.

Each saving has its price.

${overText
    {' {giHalfResolutionRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "A moving light must relight the room, coarse cones blur thin walls, and half resolution frays the edges"}.

${overText {' {giHalfResolutionRange 1.0 0.0}} "What is left is to turn the buffers into colour"}.

### Where to read {#c8-sources kind=2d sources="update-gate, light-cache, relight-gate, sort-cells, level-dispatch, local-size, pass-order, raymarch, light-at-pos, cones-low, cones-high, quality-doc, spec-cone, half-res-setting, half-res-buffers, half-res-shift, half-res-dispatch, gi-buffer-fetch, half-res-doc"}

These are the lines that keep it fast, from the gate to the half-size buffers.

## Into the final image {#c9 number=9 target=180 summary="The opaque pass mixes the environment toward VoxelGI's light by its alpha; one pixel followed back; three takeaways." sources="gi-buffer-mix, forward-gi-transparent, forward-cones, fog-inject, debug-noop, bias-mismatch"}

### The opaque pass {#c9-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="CONE, OPAQUE"}

Now the opaque pass turns the screen pass's two buffers into colour.

One buffer holds the ambient light, and the other holds the reflection.

### Mixed with the environment by alpha {#c9-mix kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelValues 0.5 {' {viewMode 0.0} {interior 0.0} {focusRow 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 36.0 14.0} {aim 46.0 18.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-buffer-mix"}}}${over
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    {overText {' {pixelAzimuthRange 60.0 90.0}} "The ambient buffer holds the cones' light"}}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "Its alpha holds how much of the view the cones found filled"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 3.0 0.0}}
    "A cone that leaves the box finds nothing, so its alpha stays low"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "Where the alpha is low, the opaque pass mixes toward the environment's own ambient light, which here comes from the sky"}.

${overText
    {' {pixelAzimuthRange 90.0 75.0}}
    "The reflection is mixed with the sky's reflection the same way"}.

${overText
    {' {focusRowRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "So the sky still lights what the cones saw as empty, here through the room's open top and front"}.

${overText
    {' {interiorRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "With the interior setting on, the alpha is one, so the sky never mixes in"}.

${overText
    {' {interiorRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "After that, the mixed light is treated like any other ambient light"}.

${over
    {' {focusRowRange 3.0 7.0}}
    {overText
        {' {pixelAzimuthRange 75.0 60.0}}
        "In turn, the opaque pass applies ambient occlusion, the albedo, screen-space effects, and the environment's reflectance to it"}}.

### Transparent surfaces and fog read it too {#c9-others kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {' {voxelgiMix 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 14.0 16.0 6.6} {pose 28.0 20.0 6.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-gi-transparent"}}}${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Transparent surfaces cannot use the buffers, and there are none in this room"}.

${overText
    {' {qualityRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Instead, each transparent surface traces its own four cones in the forward pass that draws it, at every quality setting"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "fog-inject"}}}${over
    {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.0}}
    {overText
        {' {qualityRange 1.0 0.0}}
        "Volumetric fog reads every mip of the texture at each point of its grid, with no cone at all"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "debug-noop"}}}${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.75}}
    "The editor's lighting view also reads the texture, but it never applies the dynamic range"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.75 0.5}}
    "So at the default range of two, that view shows the light at half its strength"}.

### One pixel, followed back {#c9-follow kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel panelHidden 0.3 {'
    {workedPixel 1.0}
    {conesShown 4.0}
    {specCone 0.0}
    {coneSteps 16.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 36.0 16.0 3.2} {pose 24.0 22.0 3.6} {pose 32.0 18.0 3.0}}}}}}}

${overText
    {' {pixelElevationRange 10.0 20.0}}
    "Follow this pixel on the sphere back through it all"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 16.0 2.0}} "Its cones read the texture's mips around it"}.

${cue "key" {' demoMips {slice 2.0} {mipLevel 0.0} {workedParent 1.0} panelHidden}}${overText
    {' {mipLevelRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "The mip pass built those mips from the leaves"}.

${cue "key" {' demoBounce {workedBounce 1.0} {conesShown 0.0} {coneSteps 6.0} panelHidden}}${overText
    {' {conesShownRange 0.0 6.0}}
    "Those leaves hold the panel's direct light, plus one bounce"}.

${overText {' {coneStepsRange 1.0 6.0}} "Each leaf gathered that bounce with six cones of its own"}.

${cue "key" {'
    demoOctree
    {workedTriangle 1.0}
    {workedLeaf 1.0}
    {leafColumns 0.0}
    {octreeLevel -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.01 0.24 -1.71}}
    {cameraDistance 0.4}
    panelHidden}}${over
        {' {leafColumnsRange 0.0 16.0}}
        {overText
            {' {descentLevelRange 5.0 7.0}}
            "And each leaf is the average of the samples the bake took from the room's triangles"}}.

### Three takeaways {#c9-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden 1.0 {' {voxelgiMix 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {'
    {motion {' {cameraPath {' {pose 16.0 16.0 6.8} {pose 26.0 20.0 6.2} {pose 20.0 18.0 6.4}}}}}}}

${overText {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}} "The first takeaway is the data"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "A sparse octree is baked once, then poured into a dense texture with one mip per level"}.

${overText {' {voxelgiMixRange 1.0 0.4}} "The second is the light"}.

${overText
    {' {viewModeRange 3.0 1.0}}
    "Light passes run when the lights change, and then every pixel traces cones that read wider mips as they go"}.

${overText {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.4 0.6}} "The third is the trade"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.6 0.8}}
    "The static scene must be baked, and moving objects are only drawn in, frame by frame"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelgiMixRange 0.8 1.0}}
    "The cells are five centimetres wide, and the cones see the room only as coarse averages"}.

${overText {' {viewModeRange 1.0 0.5}} "Area lights cast no shadows"}.

${overText {' {viewModeRange 0.5 0.0}} "In return, a pixel needs only a few cone samples"}.

### Where to read {#c9-sources kind=2d sources="gi-buffer-mix, forward-gi-transparent, forward-cones, fog-inject, debug-noop, bias-mismatch"}

These are the lines of the opaque pass's mix, and those of the other passes that read the texture.

